\section*{Problem \#1}

Let $S$ be the set of cards, and $|S| = n$.

If $n = 1$, return that card.

If $n = 2$, test if they are equivalent, and return either if so.

If $n > 2$, let $S_1$ be the set of the first $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ cards (half the cards), and let $S_2$ be the second half.

Run $S_1$ through the algorithm recursively. If a card is returned, then test this against the others. If a card for which there is majority equivalence has not been found, then run $S_2$ through the algorithm.

If a card is returned from $S_2$, test this against the others. If a card for which majority equivalence is found, return it (in which case the answer is yes).

\subsection*{Correctness}

The algorithm looks for a card that is equivalent to half of the cards. If there are more than $n/2$ equivalent cards, then at least one of the two sets $S_1$ or $S_2$ will have more than half the cards also equivalent, so at least one of the two recursive calls will return an equivalent card.

\subsection*{Efficiency / Optimality / Running Time}

Let $T(n)$ be the maximum number of tests for $n$ cards. There are 2 recursive calls and an additional $2n$ tests:
\[
T(n) \leq 2T(n/2) + 2n
\]
By Master's Theorem, this is $O(n \log n)$.

\section*{Problem \#2}

Check whether the middle element is a lucky entry. If it is, return it. If not, check if the index of the middle element is greater than the value of the element.

If so, then the entry might be to the right of the middle element if it exists. If not, the lucky entry might be to the left.

Run either half through the algorithm recursively. If none exists, return \texttt{NONE}.

\subsection*{Correctness}

We use strong induction on $n$.

\textbf{Base case ($n = 1$):} The algorithm checks if this is lucky and returns it if so; otherwise it returns \texttt{NONE}.

\textbf{Inductive Hypothesis:} If there is a lucky entry, the algorithm will return it; otherwise it will return \texttt{NONE}.

If the middle entry is not lucky, there are two cases:

\textbf{Case 1:} index of middle entry $>$ middle element.

Because the array is sorted, a lucky entry, if it exists, must be between the middle element and element $n$. This gets searched recursively and by the inductive hypothesis will return a lucky entry if it exists.

\textbf{Case 2:} middle entry $<$ middle element.

Similarly, a lucky entry must be between $0$ and the middle element, and this gets searched recursively.

\subsection*{Efficiency}

This is $O(\log n)$ because the array gets repeatedly split in half with each recursive iteration:
\[
n,\quad \frac{n}{2},\quad \frac{n}{2^2},\quad \frac{n}{2^3},\quad \dots,\quad \frac{n}{2^k}
\]

Eventually the length becomes 1:
\[
1 = \frac{n}{2^k}
\]
so
\[
n = 2^k
\]
and therefore
\[
\log n = \log 2^k = k
\]

\section*{Problem \#3}

We will iterate through the rows/columns until we find the row/column with the least amount of arrows pointing out of that row/column.

For every column $j \geq 0$, for every row $i < n$, if we find an arrow pointing at an element ``Billy,'' choose that element. Otherwise, check if the arrow is pointing in that column or out.

The row/column with the least amount of arrows pointing out is the one with Billy.

\subsection*{Efficiency}

This is $O(n)$ because there is only one traversal, $i$ from $0$ to $n$ and $j$ from $0$ to $n$.

\subsection*{Correctness}

``Billy'' is in a spot with an arrow pointing at him. We use induction on $n$.

\textbf{Base case:} In a $1 \times 1$, there are zero arrows pointing out, so row 1 column 1 contains Billy. The algorithm will return that.

\textbf{Inductive Hypothesis:} Assume the algorithm finds Billy in an $n \times n$.

Add another row/column and say Billy is in it. Otherwise, by induction the algorithm already found him.

He's in all $n+1$ rows and columns now are the columns with the least amount of arrows pointing out because the row/column containing Billy always contains less.